From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 21:21:58 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: hold a Darwin system suspend until a wake source fires

A system suspend clears O_ASYNC on every descriptor that is not a wake
source, so on a Linux host only a wake source's SIGIO ends the sleep in
um_suspend_enter().  Darwin's SIGIO is the IRQ notification thread, and
its os_clear_fd_async() cleared only O_NONBLOCK: every registered
descriptor still woke the suspended CPU, and the first network packet,
console byte or vhost-user backend request ended the suspend with no
wake request.

Make a descriptor's O_ASYNC its registration on the notification
queue.  os_clear_fd_async() removes it and os_set_fd_async() adds it
back with the descriptor's current events, so an edge that arrived
meanwhile notifies at once; the dispatch queue keeps every edge, so
nothing that arrives during the suspend is lost.

A suspended descriptor stays nonblocking.  Darwin still reads a vhost-user
backend's request socket while interrupts are suspended: a reply wait
serves the backend's requests, and the irq_work that delivers what one
recorded drains the socket again, until it would block.  With
O_NONBLOCK cleared that read held the CPU in recvmsg() for as long as
the backend kept sending, and the suspend never ended.

A Darwin idle sleep also ends for host signals and for an edge the
notification thread collected before the suspend cleared its
descriptor.  As suspend-to-idle does, um_suspend_enter() now sleeps
until a wake source has input or a wakeup is pending.

A management-console wake that arrives before the suspend has stopped
interrupts is answered at once and never reaches that sleep, so it used
to leave the guest suspended until the next request.  The wake now
reports a wakeup, which aborts a suspend at the PM core's next check
from the moment that suspend began, file system sync included, whether
the host or the guest started it, and um_suspend_enter() does not sleep
after one.  A wake that arrives after the host asked for a suspend but
before the suspend began counts until the request ends.  A guest that
is not suspending only answers, and its next suspend discards the
wakeup, so a host may send the wake whenever its user presses a power
key.

A wake source of the guest's own, such as an RTC alarm, that fires
while a suspend the host asked for is being entered now ends that
suspend too, with -EBUSY.  The host still wants the guest suspended,
and may have stopped waiting for the reply, so a request whose suspend
ended with -EBUSY enters it again, at most three more times, unless
the host's own wake has arrived.
